# GuiOS dashboard (F1): the view the chrome test deliberately does not cover.
#
# What this adds over desktop-chrome is the gauge. The memory gauge is one of
# the widgets the GuiOS chrome work was about -- it never showed a value at all,
# because gr-render-gauge drew its fill at the track's own depth and the
# rasterizer discards a rect at a depth it has already written. So the track's
# extent, its border and its position are worth a golden even though the FILL
# WIDTH is not comparable.
#
# The mask covers the three fields that are not a function of the program:
#
#   Uptime: <ticks/18>s     host-driven; at an 11s deadline it divides within
#                           one tick of a boundary and flips 10/11 under load
#   Memory: <heap>MB        allocator high-water at capture time
#   the gauge FILL edge     driven by that same heap figure
#
# The rect is MEASURED, not eyeballed: the same frame captured at an 11000 ms
# and a 20000 ms deadline with the clock frozen differs by 829 pixels, whose
# bounding box is x=268..349, y=88..139. Re-derive it the same way if the
# layout moves -- and it DID move: this read x=203..268 over 278 pixels until
# GuiOS stopped synthesizing a font and started drawing with the one packaged
# into the OS, which changed every text metric on the frame.
#
# The mask is that box with a few pixels of margin, and the margin is not
# padding-for-comfort: the heap figure can change digit COUNT between runs
# (1 MB against 100 MB is a wider string), and a mask sized to one run's
# digits would then let the extra digit fail the comparison. The cost of the
# margin is that it also clips the tail of the words `Uptime:` and `Memory:`,
# so a regression that garbled those two labels inside the box would not be
# caught. That is the trade, stated rather than hidden; the labels are also
# spelled out in the static text above, which IS compared.
#
# Everything else in the frame is compared, including the gauge track, its
# border, both separators, the whole keyboard-shortcuts block, the frozen
# clock line, the sidebar and the taskbar. The harness prints the masked area
# on every run so the hole stays visible: 4560 pixels, 0.58% of the frame.
#
# Controls, both run rather than assumed. Positive: this frame captured at a
# 20000 ms deadline -- a different uptime -- compares clean against a golden
# recorded at 11000 ms, which is what proves the mask covers the volatile
# region. Negative: the same run with the mask line removed differs by 278
# pixels, which is what proves the mask is load-bearing and not decoration.

# The mask hides the VALUE; expect-ink keeps the area from being wholly
# unchecked. It counts lit pixels rather than reading the text, so it is a
# heuristic and nothing more -- but before it, a regression that blanked the
# uptime and memory lines entirely, or filled them with garbage, passed this
# golden silently, because the only thing covering that rect was the mask.
#
# The range is measured, not chosen: the field reads 271 lit pixels at the
# 11000 ms deadline and 816 at 20000, rising steeply because the heap gauge's
# fill edge is inside the rect and grows with the heap. 150..1400 covers the
# retry span (which reaches 18500 ms) with room.
#
# Be clear about which end of that range does the work. The LOWER bound is the
# real check: a field that stopped drawing reads near zero and fails. The upper
# bound is loose on purpose, because the quantity inside the rect legitimately
# triples over the span the retries can reach, and a tight ceiling would fail
# on a slow host rather than on a defect. This is a heuristic and that is what
# a heuristic looks like when it is described honestly.
#
# Reading the VALUE is now possible in principle and was not before: GuiOS drew
# through a synthesized font that rendered 's', 't' and 'b' as one identical
# bitmap. It now uses the font packaged with the OS -- an 8x16 bitmap, 128
# glyphs, fixed 9-pixel advance -- so a glyph atlas is tractable and an
# expect-text worth building. This stays as the cheap check until it is.

vmargs -rtc 2026-07-21T09:41:07
mask 264 84 92 60
expect-ink 264 84 92 60 150 1400

boot 9000
settle 2000
